(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which set of features characterizes a benign tumor?","choices":["Autonomous growth, clonal origin, parenchyma and stroma, a single genetic alteration, no stimulus needed","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses, abnormal differentiation, and invasion","Circumscribed, compresses, often encapsulated, well differentiated, no metastasis, slow","Ragged border, infiltrates surrounding tissue, no capsule, variably differentiated, may metastasize, grows rapidly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — each has a malignant counterpart: a ragged border, infiltration and invasion, various degrees of differentiation, possible metastasis, and rapid growth. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which tinea subtype affects the nails?","choices":["Tinea unguium, also called onychomycosis","Tinea capitis, also called onychomycosis","Tinea manuum, affecting the nails of the hand","Tinea barbae, affecting nails and beard"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Corporis is trunk and limbs, pedis foot, cruris groin, capitis head, facialis face, manuum hand, barbae beard. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which mechanism explains the ototoxicity of gentamicin and tobramycin?","choices":["They cross-link DNA in the stria vascularis, disturbing endolymph ion homeostasis","Reactive oxygen species destroy outer hair cells from the base, so high pitches go first","They alter the stria vascularis potential reversibly","They inhibit the prestin motor protein in outer hair cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides induce reactive oxygen species that selectively destroy outer hair cells, starting at the cochlear base, so high-pitch hearing is lost first. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the hallmark of dry, atrophic macular degeneration?","choices":["Cotton-wool spots in the nerve fiber layer","Drusen: yellow extracellular debris beneath the pigment epithelium","Choroidal neovascularization breaching upward into the subretinal space","Neovascularization on the optic disc"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — drusen are discrete yellow extracellular deposits of lipofuscin and apolipoproteins beneath the pigment epithelium and Bruch membrane, the hallmark of the dry form. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which statement correctly identifies the carcinogens produced by the combustion of tobacco?","choices":["Aromatic amines, causing bladder and lung cancer; among the most powerful carcinogens known","Nitrosamines, causing gastric and esophageal cancer","Aflatoxins, causing hepatocellular carcinoma","Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, causing bladder and lung cancer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — among the most powerful carcinogens known, and tobacco smoke contains numerous others besides. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"The term PMN (polymorphonuclear leukocyte) refers to which cells?","choices":["All granulocytes","Neutrophils only","All mononuclear leukocytes","Plasma cells and memory B cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphonuclear leukocyte, abbreviated PMN, is used to refer to all granulocytes rather than to neutrophils alone. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which pairing of removal mechanism and result of failed removal is correct for the meshwork of fibrinous inflammation?","choices":["Complement fragment C3b; failure results in ulceration","Macrophage digestion; failure results in gangrene","Phagocytosis by neutrophils; failure produces pus","Fibrolytic enzymes; failure produces scar tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The meshwork can only be removed by fibrolytic enzymes, and failure of this results in scar tissue formation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which statement describes familial adenomatosis polyposis syndrome?","choices":["Hundreds to thousands of premalignant polyps young, then colon cancer by fifty","Patients develop a single premalignant polyp in childhood, and colon cancer by age thirty","Patients develop multiple neurofibromas at puberty, and sarcoma by age forty","Patients develop skin cancer in sun-exposed areas from early childhood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — five hundred to two thousand five hundred adenomatous polyps in the teens and twenties. The responsible gene is adenomatous polyposis coli. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is a biomaterial?","choices":["Anything not human that is put into a human","Any material that has been sterilized before implantation","Any tissue harvested from the patient's own body","Any protein produced during the inflammatory response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A biomaterial is anything not human put into a human, and it becomes coated with immunoglobulin G and C3b once implanted. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What can a myopic patient see well?","choices":["Objects up close","Objects far away","Neither near nor far without correction","Only objects at the extreme periphery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — myopia is nearsightedness, so near vision is the preserved one. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A temporal lobe lesion affecting part of the optic radiation produces which defect?","choices":["Contralateral inferior quadrantanopsia instead","Bitemporal hemianopsia","Monocular blindness","Contralateral superior quadrantanopsia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a temporal lobe lesion, surgery or middle cerebral artery stroke at the temporal optic radiation causes contralateral superior quadrantanopsia, pie in the sky. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the two phases of allergic contact dermatitis?","choices":["Irritation, then hyperkeratosis of the stratum corneum","Radial growth, then vertical growth","Sensitization, as Langerhans cells present haptens to T cells to form memory; then elicitation, as re-exposure drives T cell migration and inflammation","Sensitization by mast cell degranulation, followed by elicitation through the release of histamine into the surrounding dermal tissue on every subsequent exposure to that same allergen thereafter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Langerhans cells train T cells against a hapten, and re-exposure triggers T cell migration and inflammation, a delayed reaction at 48 to 72 hours. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the sequence by which Eustachian tube dysfunction creates negative middle ear pressure?","choices":["The tympanic membrane becomes stiff and pulls the ossicles inward","The tube fails to open, so the mucosa absorbs the gases","Mucus production increases faster than the tube can drain it","Bacterial infection erodes the tube lining and opens it permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The middle ear is an air-filled space; if it is not re-ventilated, the mucosa absorbs the gases and the pressure falls. That negative pressure is the start of the otitis media sequence. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which set of causes belongs to sensorineural rather than conductive loss?","choices":["Eustachian tube dysfunction, middle ear effusion and stapes fixation","Presbycusis, ototoxic drugs, noise trauma and acoustic neuroma","Cerumen impaction, otosclerosis, otitis media and tympanic membrane perforation","Cholesteatoma, exostoses, foreign body and ossicular necrosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All four damage the cochlea or the nerve. Note acoustic neuroma sits here because cranial nerve VIII is part of the sensorineural pathway even though the tumor is outside the cochlea. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which protein deficiency contributes to atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Insufficient elastin, reflecting loss of recoil","Insufficient keratohyalin, reflecting loss of the granular layer","Insufficient filaggrin, reflecting a compromised epidermal barrier","Insufficient collagen, reflecting fibroblast failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Insufficient filaggrin, due to a compromised epidermal barrier, combines with an overactive immune system and genetic, environmental and psychogenic factors. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which dermal cell mediates immunoglobulin E-mediated inflammation?","choices":["The fibroblast","The melanocyte","The keratinocyte","The mast cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and its degranulation is the mechanism behind both the wheal and urticaria. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Trauma versus a bee sting illustrates which distinction within acute inflammation?","choices":["Inflammation that resolves completely versus inflammation that becomes chronic","Serous versus fibrinous exudate","Endogenous versus exogenous toxic agents","Purely physical damage versus damage that activates an immune response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In acute inflammation, damage may be purely physical, as in trauma, or may involve activation of an immune response, as in a bee sting. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which mediator is distinguished from histamine and serotonin by also producing pain?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G","Fibrinogen","Bradykinin","Complement component C3b"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three raise permeability, but only bradykinin also causes pain, along with a possible increase in leukocyte chemotaxis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What defines a secondary skin lesion?","choices":["Any lesion that is palpable rather than flat","Any lesion that arises from malignant rather than from benign underlying disease processes","A modification or change of a primary lesion caused by infection, trauma or other factors","Any lesion appearing on a second body site after the first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why a secondary lesion may or may not still resemble what it came from. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Is metastatic spread random?","choices":["No — but only because lymphatic drainage is anatomically fixed","No — it is determined by blood flow, receptors, and genetically determined fitness","Yes — circulating cells lodge in whichever capillary bed they reach first","Yes for hematogenous spread, no for lymphatic spread"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — metastatic spread is not random but set by venous blood flow, tumor and endothelial receptors, and genetic fitness. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What do B lymphocytes do?","choices":["Cell-mediated killing of virus-infected cells through T cell receptors","Returning immune function to its normal operation after infection, and preventing autoimmunity","Releasing histamine to increase vascular permeability","Making antibodies, presenting antigen, and becoming memory B cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. B lymphocytes make antibodies, act as antigen-presenting cells and become memory B cells; they drive the humoral immune response. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which two ototoxic effects are reversible or symptom-limited rather than permanent?","choices":["Noise trauma and presbycusis","Furosemide and salicylate","Gentamicin and tobramycin damage","Cisplatin and carboplatin damage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The loop diuretic effect on the strial potential is reversible, and the salicylate effect on the outer hair cell motor protein produces tinnitus. Neither destroys the cells outright. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the inherited defect in xeroderma pigmentosum?","choices":["Defective apoptosis genes, so damaged cells cannot self destruct; increased risk of lymphoma","Defective protooncogenes, so growth signaling is lost; increased risk of aplasia","Defective repair genes, so ultraviolet B damage cannot be repaired","Defective tumor suppressor genes, so cell growth cannot be inhibited; increased risk of nervous system tumors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the consequence is an increased risk of skin cancer in sun-exposed areas. It is a repair-gene disease rather than a tumor suppressor one. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In the organ of Corti, what physically opens the tip-link channels on the hair cells?","choices":["Shearing of the stereocilia against the tectorial membrane","Stapes vibration transmitted directly to the hair cells","Direct pressure of endolymph on the hair cell body","Glutamate binding to the hair cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Basilar membrane displacement bends the stereocilia against the rigid tectorial membrane, and that mechanical deflection is what opens the channels. Everything upstream exists to produce that shear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which pairing of infection type and predominant reaction describes the eosinophil?","choices":["Bacterial infection; predominating in suppurative inflammation","Parasitic infection; predominating in allergic reactions such as asthma","Viral infection; predominating in cell-mediated immunity","Fungal infection; predominating in chronic granulomatous inflammatory processes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eosinophils primarily deal with parasitic infections and are the predominant inflammatory cells in allergic reactions such as asthma, hay fever and hives. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which three structures make up the uvea?","choices":["The choroid, the retina and the sclera","The ciliary body, the lens and the zonules","The iris, the cornea and the conjunctiva","The choroid, the ciliary body and the iris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the choroid, ciliary body and iris form the uvea, the vascular middle tunic, with the highly vascularized choroid nourishing the retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Eczematous dermatitis results from which type of inflammatory reaction?","choices":["Neutrophil-driven suppurative inflammation","Complement-mediated vessel destruction","T cell-mediated","Immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell degranulation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Eczematous dermatitis typically results from T cell-mediated inflammatory reactions, with insufficient filaggrin compromising the epidermal barrier. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is a carcinoma showing squamous cell differentiation called?","choices":["A papilloma","A sarcoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","An adenocarcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a carcinoma with squamous cell differentiation is a squamous cell carcinoma; one with glandular growth is an adenocarcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What are the two pathophysiological causes of strabismus?","choices":["Lens opacification and loss of the red reflex","Extraocular muscle imbalance and third, fourth or sixth nerve palsy","Retinal detachment and vitreous hemorrhage","Raised intraocular pressure with progressive optic disc cupping and field loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — strabismus is mechanical: an extraocular muscle imbalance or a palsy of the third, fourth or sixth cranial nerve misaligns the visual axes. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is amblyopia?","choices":["Ocular misalignment arising from extraocular muscle imbalance or nerve palsy","Progressive optic neuropathy from raised pressure","Opacification of the crystalline lens","Reduced best-corrected acuity from abnormal visual development"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — amblyopia is a visual deficit rather than a mechanical one: best-corrected acuity stays reduced because visual processing developed abnormally during the critical pediatric period. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What drives the change from non-proliferative to proliferative diabetic retinopathy?","choices":["Rising intraocular pressure forces new vessels to form","Accumulation of lipofuscin and apolipoprotein beneath the pigment epithelium","Bacterial collagenase digestion of the retinal surface","Severe ischemia upregulates vascular endothelial growth factor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — chronic hyperglycemia occludes capillaries, severe ischemia upregulates vascular endothelial growth factor, and fragile new vessels grow on the disc and retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which statement correctly defines cancer and gives the origin of its name?","choices":["A neoplasm that has metastasized; from the Greek for wandering, because cells migrate","A precancerous lesion; from the Latin for disorder, because architecture is lost","A malignant neoplasm; from the Latin for crab","Any neoplasm at all; from the Latin for growth, because the mass enlarges without a stimulus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — so named because it adheres to any tissue it seizes upon and reaches out with claws, invading surrounding tissue. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where do impulses travel after leaving the optic nerve?","choices":["To the temporal cortex","To the frontal eye fields","To the superior colliculus alone","To the occipital cortex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the signal runs from the optic nerve through the chiasm and optic tract to the lateral geniculate nucleus, then via the optic radiations to the occipital cortex. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What drives dizziness or lightheadedness, as distinct from vertigo?","choices":["Cerebral hypoperfusion or metabolic imbalance","Asymmetrical input between the two vestibular apparatus","Dislodged otoconia in the semicircular canals","Endolymphatic hydrops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dizziness is a non-vestibular sensation of impending faint or unsteadiness, driven by cerebral hypoperfusion, orthostatic hypotension or metabolic imbalance. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why are there three semicircular canals rather than one?","choices":["Each detects a different speed of rotation","Two detect rotation and one detects gravity","Redundancy in case one is damaged","They cover three planes of rotation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sagittal, coronal and transverse between them cover all rotational movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the conjunctival fornix?","choices":["The dense fibrous plate giving the eyelid its shape","The sphincteric muscle responsible for blinking","The circular junction where the conjunctiva meets the edge of the cornea","A flexible junctional cul-de-sac that permits free rotation of the globe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the fornix is the flexible junctional cul-de-sac where palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva meet, and its slack permits free rotation of the globe. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is a papilloma?","choices":["A tumor with finger-like or warty projections from an epithelial surface","A tumor forming a glandular pattern or arising from a gland","A malignant tumor arising from supportive, mesenchymal tissue","A malignant epithelial tumor showing squamous cell differentiation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the projections are visible microscopically or macroscopically. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the cellular events of acute inflammation after stasis?","choices":["Transmigration, adhesion, aggregation, phagocytosis, chemotaxis","Aggregation, chemotaxis, transmigration, adhesion, phagocytosis","Leukocyte adhesion, transmigration, chemotaxis, aggregation, phagocytosis","Chemotaxis, phagocytosis, adhesion, transmigration, aggregation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adhesion to the endothelium precedes movement across it, then directed migration, gathering at the site, and finally ingestion. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are mesenchymal tumors derived from?","choices":["Neural tissue: peripheral nerves, nerve sheaths and the central nervous system","Supportive tissue: connective tissue, adipose tissue, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone","Epithelial surfaces: skin, gastrointestinal lining, respiratory lining and glandular ducts","Hematopoietic tissue: bone marrow, lymph nodes, spleen and thymus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same origin gives sarcomas when the tumor is malignant. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which histological findings go with chronic inflammation?","choices":["Lymphocytes and macrophages, blood vessel proliferation, fibrosis and tissue necrosis","Neutrophil predominance with an absence of vascular proliferation","Eosinophil infiltration with mucus hypersecretion","Serous exudate with low protein content, no cellular infiltrate, and no proliferation of blood vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic inflammation is associated histologically with lymphocytes and macrophages, new blood vessels, fibrosis and tissue necrosis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which demographic and inheritance profile fits otosclerosis?","choices":["Young-to-middle-aged females; 50% autosomal dominant","Commonest in young females but entirely sporadic","Commonest in older males, with no hereditary component","Commonest in children, autosomal recessive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Otosclerosis is most common in young-to-middle-aged females and is accelerated by pregnancy; 50 percent of cases are autosomal dominant with variable penetrance. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which nodal findings require immediate assessment to exclude lymphoma?","choices":["Persistent rubbery or matted nodes in older adults","Any palpable cervical node at all","Tender mobile nodes during a viral upper respiratory infection","Bilateral small nodes in a child with otitis media"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Reactive nodes are usually tender and follow a regional infection. Rubbery, matted, persistent and supraclavicular in an older patient is the combination that changes the level of concern. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which genes promote repair of damaged deoxyribonucleic acid?","choices":["BRCA-1 and BRCA-2","NF-1 and NF-2","Retinoblastoma and adenomatous polyposis coli","p16 and p53"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a minority of breast cancer patients carry an inherited mutation in one of them. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which glands are involved in an external as opposed to an internal hordeolum?","choices":["Both involve the tarsal plate itself rather than any gland","External involves the glands of Zeis or Moll; internal involves a meibomian gland","External involves a meibomian gland; internal involves the glands of Zeis or Moll","Both involve the lacrimal gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an external hordeolum is an acute abscess of the Zeis or Moll glands at the lash follicles, and an internal one involves a meibomian gland in the tarsal plate. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In chronic inflammation, what roles do lymphocytes and plasma cells versus macrophages play?","choices":["Both populations act solely to proliferate blood vessels","Lymphocytes and plasma cells make antibody; macrophages process and deliver antigen","Lymphocytes release histamine; macrophages release serotonin","Lymphocytes phagocytose the cellular debris at the site, while macrophages produce and secrete the antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibody production belongs to lymphocytes and plasma cells, antigen processing and delivery to macrophages. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A patient reacts to a nickel earring 48 to 72 hours after wearing it. Which mechanism does the delay reflect?","choices":["An immediate immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell degranulation occurring upon the very first contact with the metal","Direct chemical injury requiring no prior exposure","Viral proliferation within basal keratinocytes","Delayed hypersensitivity: T cells migrate after re-exposure to a hapten once presented by Langerhans cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The delay is the time the cellular response takes, which is what distinguishes it from an immediate mast cell reaction. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the suspected mechanism of koilonychia?","choices":["Overgrowth of connective tissue producing an opaque nail","Incomplete keratinization producing white spots","Impaired keratin synthesis with deficiency anemia, leaving a thin, concave nail plate","A halt of keratin production producing horizontal transverse grooves across the surface of the nail plate itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Koilonychia is thought to reflect impaired keratin synthesis with deficiency anemia, leaving a weakened, thin, concave nail; the exact pathophysiology is unknown. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What does anaplasia mean?","choices":["Disordered growth of epithelium with retained architecture","Lack of differentiation","Close resemblance to the comparable normal cell","Failure of a primordial tissue to develop into a mature organ"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the extreme of the grading scale, with no resemblance to the normal cell. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which condition can cause amblyopia?","choices":["Raised intraocular pressure","Posterior vitreous detachment","Drusen beneath the retinal pigment epithelium","Uncorrected strabismus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — uncorrected strabismus is a cause of amblyopia, the loss of best-corrected acuity from abnormal visual processing in the critical pediatric period. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes Epstein Barr virus in carcinogenesis?","choices":["B cell lymphomas and nasopharyngeal carcinoma; it infects B lymphocytes","Hepatocellular carcinoma; it infects hepatocytes and causes chronic injury with regeneration","Cervical carcinoma; it infects cervical epithelium and integrates its genome","Gastric adenocarcinoma and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma; it colonizes the stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it immortalizes them, which is the key step, and it also infects epithelial cells of the oropharynx. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the primary etiology of TRACTIONAL retinal detachment?","choices":["Choroidal melanoma","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Severe malignant hypertension","Posterior vitreous detachment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the neovascular membranes of proliferative disease are the classic traction source. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which description of an epidermal inclusion cyst is accurate?","choices":["A sebaceous gland, and it contains only squalene","A blood vessel, and it contains clotted blood","A hair follicle, and it contains keratin","A sweat gland, and it contains sodium and chloride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An epidermal inclusion cyst arises from a hair follicle and contains keratin, enclosed in a capsule within the dermis or subcutaneous layer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which description fits vocal cord polyps, as distinct from nodules?","choices":["Unilateral fibrous calluses from chronic yelling","Bilateral fibrous calluses from chronic phonotrauma","Bilateral soft lesions at the anterior commissure from reflux","Unilateral, soft, pedunculated; from one severe strain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One event rather than chronic repetition, and the healing response is inflammatory rather than a callus. Polyps sit on the middle third, so site and laterality both differ from nodules. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why can an implanted medical device provoke an inflammatory response even when it is sterile?","choices":["Because implantation always causes bacterial contamination of surrounding tissue","Because sterilization leaves residual endotoxin behind, and that endotoxin then acts as a chemotactic agent","Because it becomes coated with immunoglobulin G and C3b, which immune cells recognize","Because the device physically obstructs lymphatic drainage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Opsonization of the biomaterial is what makes it a target regardless of sterility. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which mechanism drives psoriasis?","choices":["Melanocytes proliferate at the dermal-epidermal junction","Active T cells in the epidermis drive keratinocyte proliferation and excess cytokines, such as tumor necrosis factor alpha","Mast cells release histamine which drives keratinocyte proliferation","Langerhans cells present haptens to T cells, producing a delayed hypersensitivity reaction 48 to 72 hours after each exposure to the allergen in question every single time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Active T cells infiltrate the epidermis, stimulating keratinocyte proliferation and excess cytokines (tumor necrosis factor alpha, interferon gamma and interleukin-12), so skin cells multiply up to ten times faster. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which molecular pathway drives nasal polyp formation?","choices":["Interleukins 4, 5 and 13, with eosinophil influx","Interleukin 17, with mast cell influx","Interleukins 1, 6 and tumor necrosis factor, with neutrophil influx","Interferon gamma, with lymphocyte influx"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic type 2 allergic responses release interleukins 4, 5 and 13 and draw eosinophils into the tissue, forming benign edematous masses from the sinus ostia or ethmoid air cells. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which description fits inferior turbinate hypertrophy?","choices":["Bacterial infection of the turbinate bone; osteomyelitis","Cartilage displacement off the midline; a congenital deformity","Venous engorgement, edema or bony hypertrophy; decongestant rebound","Eosinophilic polyp formation; an allergic reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The inferior turbinates enlarge from venous sinusoid engorgement, mucosal edema or bony hypertrophy; rebound hyperemia from topical decongestants is rhinitis medicamentosa. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How do petechiae, purpura and ecchymoses differ?","choices":["By size — petechiae 1 to 2 millimeters or less, purpura 3 to 10 millimeters, ecchymoses greater than 10 millimeters — with similar underlying pathology","By mechanism — petechiae from capillary rupture, purpura from vessel dilatation, and ecchymoses from melanin deposition in the dermis rather than blood","By depth — petechiae epidermal, purpura dermal, ecchymoses subcutaneous","By blanchability — petechiae blanch, purpura and ecchymoses do not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three are bleeding into the dermis with similar underlying pathology, separated by size, from pinpoint petechiae to ecchymoses over 10 millimeters. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes epidermal cell turnover?","choices":["Every 6 to 12 months, and considerably more rapid in older patients","Turnover ceases after adolescence","Every 30 to 60 days, and turnover is more rapid in younger patients","Every 24 to 48 hours, with no age dependence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Epidermal cells turn over every 30 to 60 days, rising from the basement membrane zone to shed, and turnover is faster in younger patients. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Pseudomembranous inflammation consists of a superficial necrotic layer made up of which components?","choices":["Granulation tissue, proliferating fibroblasts and newly formed capillary networks throughout","Fibrin, inflammatory cells and debris, forming a membrane-like covering over the affected mucosa","Pus alone, without a fibrin component","Mucus, plasma and erythrocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pseudomembranous inflammation is a superficial necrotic layer of fibrin, inflammatory cells and debris forming a membrane-like covering over mucosa. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
